Z||000000||zeusrekning||04-27-2000||11:04 PM||||It seems like I'm not the only one who is always haveing fiero problems. I spend a lot of money on my car that i don't need to be. I graduate high school in a month and will have a full time job as a CNC programer/ machinist. Do i need to become a rocket scientist or a Ceo of some corporation to own my pontiac? I love my car and hope to keep it till i get old and can tell my kids about my first real car ,a dark red 87 fiero, then take them around the block in it?    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Know to many older guys that wish they still had there firt car (ex. 57 chevy) Thanks for all the help and hope this releaves some stress for the other teens who are in the same situations.\<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.geocities.com/Baja/Mountain/4872/fiero1.jpg">  <P><BR>[This message has been edited by zeusrekning (edited 04-27-2000).]<p>[This message has been edited by zeusrekning (edited 04-27-2000).]||||reg||6
Z||000001||Linx||04-28-2000||01:08 AM||||well- yeah, but no.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>For me, it was like this.  I got my '86SE and it was a mess.  I inherited the results of someone else's poor care...and Ive had to put alot into it to make it reliable.  But eventually, it <I>did</I> get reliable. Even if i put $100 a month into it, it's still cheaper than buying new.<P>chin up. eventually youll get a handle on it and you wont minding driving it anywhere. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Standard||04-28-2000||01:10 AM||||I've been out of high school for a year, and only work part-time, and I still spend way to much money on my car.. I'm sure you can smell my check-card smoking from there.  hehe  don't worry, it'll be worth it in the end!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Homicidal||04-28-2000||01:15 AM||||My 84 SE runs GREAT, (without the exception of a new clutch).  I'm just afraid it's gonna deteriorate soon!||||reg||4
Z||000004||Phil||04-28-2000||06:14 AM||||Don't forget your are dealing with at least 12 yr. old cars usually with over 100K miles gotta expect something.||||reg||1
Z||000005||William Federle||04-28-2000||07:05 AM||||It's like any other 12-16 year old used car from the standpoint of maintenance and new parts required to keep it running. The newer car you can afford (generally speaking) the less you'll have to repair it and replace parts. One way or another, I'd have to have an inexpensive used car right now. The thing that makes it worth it for me is that the Fiero, for me, is a much more exciting used car than any other has ever been. ||||reg||1
