Z||000015||Patrick's Dad||04-29-2000||08:13 PM||||I'm definitely no millionaire. I'd try to marry one, but I don't think that my wife would like that....<P>Mt Formula is 127K miles old, and has had one new cluth, water pump, and alternator. By comparison, my wife's (POS) 1992 Explorer has 103K, leaks oil despite two fixes, leaks tranny fluid, goes through lower ball joints like no tomorrow, needs new driver door hinges (The door sags, and won't close without being lifted up first. $430.00??? For Hinges?), and, there's no routine maintenance that I've don that is any easier than the Fiero, a supposedly difficult car to wortk on. I've easily spent more on the Explorer (3 years owned) than the Fiero (9 years).<P>I can tell you, while I'd play with another Fiero in a heartbeat, this experience with the Explorer, and Ford's response, has turned me off to Ford altogether. That's why I'm gettin' me a 1973 Plymouth Valiant, with 80K!||||reg||1
Z||000016||stimpy||04-30-2000||09:56 AM||||I just bought a millionaires car, thats all! Seriously, I was fortunate enough to find a car that had a lot of that annoying stuff done on it already- dew wipes were new, suspension bits replaced, California car with no rust on the chassis... And still got it for $2500. Besides replacing my engine after it blew chunks at 135k miles and replacing rear calipers and rotors, and the cars voracious appetite for headlight motors ($20 from the scrapyard- kinda like feeding tokens into a machine)<BR>my Fiero has been relatively free of the nickle and dime bs that has always plagued cars that I have owned. (That knocking sound you hea is wood!)<BR>I got lucky buying a well maintained car when I really hadn't a clue what to look for. If I ever buy another one, thanks to all the knowledge acquired here and through members of different Fiero clubs, I will have a much better idea of what to look for.<P>(Don't want no $300 scrub Fiero!)||||reg||1
